Given lines $m$, $n$, and planes $\alpha$, $\beta$, which of the following propositions is correct?  
(1) If $m \perp \alpha$ and $m \perp \beta$, then $\alpha \perp \beta$;  
(2) If $m \parallel \alpha$ and $m \parallel \beta$, then $\alpha \parallel \beta$;  
(3) If $m \perp \alpha$ and $m \parallel \beta$, then $\alpha \perp \beta$;  
(4) If skew lines $m$, $n$ are perpendicular to each other, then there exists a plane through $m$ that is perpendicular to $n$.  
A: (2) (3)  
B: (1) (3)  
C: (2) (4)  
D: (3) (4)

s:
(1) If $m \perp \alpha$ and $m \perp \beta$, then $\alpha \perp \beta$; this proposition is incorrect because from $m \perp \alpha$ and $m \perp \beta$, it can be concluded that $\alpha \parallel \beta$, hence the proposition is wrong;  
(2) If $m \parallel \alpha$ and $m \parallel \beta$, then $\alpha \parallel \beta$; this proposition is incorrect because two planes being parallel to the same line does not guarantee that the two planes are parallel;  
(3) If $m \perp \alpha$ and $m \parallel \beta$, then $\alpha \perp \beta$; this proposition is correct because if $m \parallel \beta$, then there must exist a line $n$ in $\beta$ such that $m \parallel n$, and since $m \perp \alpha$, it can be concluded that $n \perp \alpha$. According to the theorem of perpendicularity between planes, $\alpha \perp \beta$;  
(4) If skew lines $m$, $n$ are perpendicular to each other, then there exists a plane through $m$ that is perpendicular to $n$. This proposition is correct because for any two skew lines, there exists a common perpendicular, and the plane formed by this common perpendicular and one of the lines must be perpendicular to the other line. Therefore, if skew lines $m$, $n$ are perpendicular to each other, the existence of a plane through $m$ that is perpendicular to $n$ is correct.  
In summary, propositions (3) and (4) are correct.  
Therefore, the correct choice is $\boxed{D}$.

