To solve the given expression $(\sqrt{7}-1)^2-(\sqrt{14}-\sqrt{2})(\sqrt{14}+\sqrt{2})$, we will break it down step by step:

1. First, expand the square term and the difference of squares:
   \begin{align*}
   (\sqrt{7}-1)^2-(\sqrt{14}-\sqrt{2})(\sqrt{14}+\sqrt{2}) &= (\sqrt{7}^2 - 2\cdot\sqrt{7}\cdot1 + 1^2) - ((\sqrt{14})^2 - (\sqrt{2})^2) \\
   &= 7 - 2\sqrt{7} + 1 - (14 - 2).
   \end{align*}

2. Next, simplify the expression by combining like terms and performing the subtraction:
   \begin{align*}
   7 - 2\sqrt{7} + 1 - (14 - 2) &= 7 - 2\sqrt{7} + 1 - 12 \\
   &= 8 - 2\sqrt{7} - 12 \\
   &= -4 - 2\sqrt{7}.
   \end{align*}

Therefore, the simplified form of the given expression is $\boxed{-4 - 2\sqrt{7}}$.

